---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import Prose from '../components/Prose.astro';
import { APP, REPO } from '../lib/site';

const STATEMENT =
  'https://www.tuhs.org/Archive/Distributions/Research/Dan_Cross_v8/statement_regarding_Unix_3-7-17.pdf';
---

<PageLayout
  title="Licensing — ipnx"
  description="Why ipnx is free and must remain so: the 2017 Nokia/Alcatel-Lucent statement on Research Unix Editions 8, 9 and 10, and the terms of every other component."
  eyebrow="Legal"
  heading="Licensing"
  intro="Why this is free, why it is not called Unix, and the terms every component runs under."
>
  <div class="py-8">
    <Prose>
      <p>
        This page mirrors the posture recorded in the repository, which is the authoritative
        version and carries the sources for every claim. It is not legal advice; it deliberately
        follows what the preservation community has done publicly since 2017.
      </p>

      <h2>The governing instrument</h2>
      <p>
        Research Unix Editions 8, 9 and 10 are distributable because of a statement made by
        Alcatel-Lucent, now Nokia, in March 2017:
      </p>
      <blockquote>
        <p>
          “…it will not assert its copyright rights with respect to any <strong
            >non-commercial</strong
          > copying, distribution, performance, display or creation of derivative works of Research
          Unix® Editions 8, 9, and 10.”
        </p>
      </blockquote>
      <p>Three properties of it decide everything else:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          It is a <strong>covenant not to assert, not a licence</strong> — hedged “to the extent
          of its ability to do so”, and it grants no third-party rights.
        </li>
        <li>It is <strong>non-commercial only</strong>.</li>
        <li>It covers all three editions equally.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        The statement itself is <a href={STATEMENT}>published by The Unix Heritage Society</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>What follows for this app</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>{APP.name} is free.</strong> No price, no advertising, no purchases. This is a
          condition of distributing the system at all, not a pricing strategy, and it cannot
          change while the app contains Research Unix.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>It is not called Unix.</strong> UNIX is a registered trademark of The Open
          Group and the 2017 statement grants no trademark rights, so the name and branding avoid
          it. “Edition 8”, “Research”, “Blit”, “5620” are all safe vocabulary; the product name is
          not.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>It is published personally, not by a company.</strong> A commercial publisher
          sits badly against a permission that reaches non-commercial use only, whatever the
          intent. The code signature names an enrolled Apple developer team because Apple requires
          one — that is a key-holding arrangement, not a publisher.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The disk image ships inside the app.</strong> Nothing is downloaded at runtime,
          which keeps the app self-contained.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Every component</h2>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Component</th><th>Terms</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Research Unix Edition 8 system and disk image</td>
            <td>2017 covenant — non-commercial</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Edition 10 source, and its future disk image</td>
            <td>The same covenant</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>4.1BSD-derived code inside the system</td>
            <td>UCB BSD licence — attribution in the credits</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>open-simh (the VAX)</td>
            <td>MIT</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>dmd_core (the 5620)</td>
            <td>MIT</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>AT&amp;T 5620 ROM source</td>
            <td>AT&amp;T copyright, GPL-2.0, released 1994 — <strong>read, never linked</strong></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>SwiftTerm (the glass terminals)</td>
            <td>MIT</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>The Edition 10 manual (1990)</td>
            <td>A published book, treated as encumbered — linked, never bundled</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>This project's own code</td>
            <td>MIT</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>The ROM source, and how far it was used</h2>
      <p>
        AT&amp;T published the 5620's firmware source in 1994 under the GPL, and it names the
        exact firmware this app runs. It was used to answer questions — where a structure lives,
        what a table contains — and its boundary is written down precisely in the repository:
        <strong>read, not linked</strong>. No line of it is compiled into anything shipped here.
      </p>

      <h2>The one open question, stated rather than buried</h2>
      <p>
        Edition 10's native C compiler descends from System III/V code, which sits outside both
        the 2017 covenant's power (“to the extent of its ability”) and the licence that freed the
        early editions. It remains inside the disk image, exactly as it has at TUHS since 2017 and
        unchallenged, and it is never linked into app code. It is the sharpest open item in this
        project and it is recorded as one.
      </p>

      <h2>If you are reading this to decide whether you may do something</h2>
      <p>
        The full table, the reasoning and the primary sources are in
        <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/docs/licensing.md`}>docs/licensing.md</a>, alongside the
        research file that cites each claim. If something there is wrong, please say so — it is
        the part of this project where being wrong matters most.
      </p>
    </Prose>
  </div>
</PageLayout>
